To validate an email address in a way that covers most common cases while avoiding false negatives, we can use a regex pattern that ensures:

1. **Local Part**:
   - Starts with one or more alphanumeric characters.
   - Allows special characters (`._%+-`) but only when they are **not consecutive**.
   - Ensures no two dots (`.`) appear in a row.

2. **Domain Part**:
   - Must contain at least one dot (`.`).
   - Each segment (before and after the dot) must be valid (letters, numbers, and hyphens).
   - Hyphens are allowed in the middle of segments but **not at the start or end** of a segment.

---

### ✅ Final Regex Pattern

```cpp
R"([a-zA-Z0-9]+([._%+-][a-zA-Z0-9]+)*@[a-zA-Z0-9-]+(\.[a-zA-Z0-9-]+)+)"
```

---

### ✅ C++ Implementation

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <tuple>
#include <regex>

std::tuple<bool, std::string> check_email(const std::string& email) {
    std::string pattern = R"([a-zA-Z0-9]+([._%+-][a-zA-Z0-9]+)*@[a-zA-Z0-9-]+(\.[a-zA-Z0-9-]+)+)";
    std::regex regex_pattern(pattern);
    bool is_valid = std::regex_match(email, regex_pattern);
    return {is_valid, pattern};
}
```

---

### 📌 Example Usage

```cpp
int main() {
    std::string email = "abc.123@def.ghi.com.uk";
    auto [valid, pattern] = check_email(email);
    std::cout << "Valid: " << std::boolalpha << valid << std::endl;
    std::cout << "Pattern used: " << pattern << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

---

### 📌 Notes

- This pattern is **not fully RFC-compliant**, but it covers **most common valid email formats**.
- It avoids false negatives by being **lenient** on special characters and hyphens.
- The pattern is **returned as-is**, so the caller can inspect or reuse it if needed.

